  "query_preview": "..A broad but weakening Pacific Northwest storm system\ncontinues to track across Utah and southwest Wyoming this morning.\nPrecipitation is focused over the area of best cold pool\ninstability over northern Utah, with snow levels near 5,000 feet.\nPrecipitation will continue during the day and into the\nevening, though coverage will steadily decrease during that time.\nBy this evening, remaining precipitation should be primarily\nfocused from Ogden northward, with some light precipitation\ncontinuing near the Utah/Idaho border through early Saturday\nmorning. By late evening, snow levels are expected to drop to all\nnorthern Utah valley floors, though precipitation will likely have\nended over much of the Wasatch Front by that time, limiting the\npotential for accumulating snow. Have cancelled the Winter Weather\nAdvisory for the southern mountains as precipitation has ended\nthere, but have kept remaining headlines as is for now.\n\nBehind the exiting trough, high pressure is expected to be the\ndominant feature over Utah and southwest Wyoming this weekend.\nWith things clearing quickly over southern Utah, temperatures will\ndrop noticeably tonight in an unseasonably cold airmass. This will\nbring a high chance of below freezing temperatures to areas in the\nVirgin River corridor near Zion National Park, with a good chance\nof freezing to near freezing temperatures for portions of lower\nWashington county as well Saturday morning. Temperatures are \nexpected to creep up a bit Sunday morning, but lows could stay\nnear freezing for those areas. Overall, looking at maxes 5-10F\nbelow seasonal normals Saturday and near normal to up to 5F below\nseasonal normals on Sunday.\n\nThe ridge is expected to track east of the area by late in the\nweekend, with a relatively active pattern continuing for the West\nfor the upcoming week. There is high confidence in a storm system\nimpacting the area on Monday. This will be weaker than the last\nfew systems but is likely to bring accumulating snow to the\nmountains. There is still significant model spread on some\ndetails, however, such as which areas might see the best\naccumulations. Another system is then expected to follow later in\nthe upcoming week, though details for that system are even\nmurkier.",
